2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2

The trp leader transcript can fold into alternative stem-loops (1-2 pause, 2-3 antiterminator, or 3-4 terminator). Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome reaches the stop codon and masks segment 2, freeing segment 3 to pair with 4 and form the terminator. A mutation that abolishes 1-2 pairing leaves segment 2 free to pair with segment 3 as soon as the sequences are transcribed. Formation of the 2-3 antiterminator therefore sequesters segment 3, preventing the 3-4 terminator from ever forming and allowing read-through even when tryptophan is high. The other listed mutations either strengthen termination, reduce overall transcription, or leave the 3-4 stem-loop intact.}

(The reasoning above is based solely on standard models of attenuation; no external sources were consulted.)

6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The trp leader can form three mutually exclusive RNA structures: a 1-2 pause hairpin, a 2-3 antiterminator, or a 3-4 terminator hairpin followed by the U-run. Under high tryptophan the ribosome translates trpL to its stop codon and thereby sequesters region 2, leaving regions 3 and 4 free to pair and form the terminator.  

A mutation that prevents region 1 from base-pairing with region 2 removes the competing 1-2 structure. Consequently, region 2 remains available to pair with the newly transcribed region 3, favoring formation of the 2-3 antiterminator even when tryptophan is abundant. Because the antiterminator sequesters region 3, the 3-4 terminator cannot assemble and transcription continues.  

The other options either strengthen termination (B, C), reduce overall transcription (E), or have no direct effect on hairpin choice (D). Thus only option A blocks terminator formation under repressing conditions.}

